---
// The one page under /tutorials that is not a docs/tutorials/*.md walkthrough:
// its content is the connected-session URLs that lib/jbrowseLinks.ts and
// lib/f12CombinedLinks.ts export, so markdown would have to carry a copy that
// goes stale.
// [slug].astro builds only slugs the markdown glob produces, so this file owns
// the route; lib/tutorials.ts still holds the index card.
import JBrowseFigure from '../../components/JBrowseFigure.astro'
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
import { f12CombinedCloseupUrl } from '../../lib/f12CombinedLinks'
import {
  brafV600,
  proteinLinked,
  tp53PhmmerRp15,
  tp53Protein3d,
  tp53R248,
  tp53UnirefBrowser,
} from '../../lib/jbrowseLinks'

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL
---

<DocsLayout
  title="JBrowse 2 integration — JBrowseMSA"
  description="react-msaview as a view inside JBrowse 2, connected to a genome view and an AlphaFold structure, with each session a single declarative URL."
>
  <h1>JBrowse 2 integration</h1>
  <p>
    An alignment opened in the standalone viewer stands on its own: the columns
    are the coordinate system, and nothing outside the file has a position in
    it. The
    <a href="https://github.com/GMOD/jbrowse-plugin-msaview">
      JBrowse 2 plugin
    </a>
    opens the same alignment and tree <em>inside</em> JBrowse, beside a genome
    view, and with
    <a href="https://github.com/GMOD/jbrowse-plugin-protein3d">protein3d</a>
    beside an AlphaFold structure as well. Selecting a region in any one of
    those views highlights the matching columns, codons and residues in the
    others, so a base in the genome, a column in the alignment and a residue in
    the fold are the same place.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each session below is one declarative URL: the link carries the views, the
    tracks and the data locations, so it opens the state it describes with
    nothing saved on a server. The
    <a href={`${base}/tutorials`}>tutorials</a> build the alignment and tree
    files these sessions load.
  </p>

  <JBrowseFigure
    src="f12-combined-closeup.png"
    href={f12CombinedCloseupUrl}
    alt="A single JBrowse session at base resolution over F12 exon 3: the RefSeq gene annotation track on top and the react-msaview DNA alignment with its species tree below. The four cetaceans show a gap where every other mammal has a C, the shared single-base frameshift, in the highlighted column"
    body="Genome and alignment in one JBrowse session: the F12 locus (RefSeq annotation, top) over the react-msaview DNA alignment and species tree, zoomed to base resolution at exon 3. The two views sit side by side without a connection, so a selection in one highlights nothing in the other. The highlighted column holds the single-base deletion the four cetaceans carry, a gap where every other mammal has a C, which disables the gene. The 14-exon model is registered to the alignment columns and lifted onto each species."
  />

  <JBrowseFigure
    src="genome-browser-tp53-protein3d.png"
    href={tp53Protein3d}
    alt="JBrowse: the TP53 gene with ClinVar, the p53 ortholog alignment, and the AlphaFold structure, all connected, with the nuclear export signal motif highlighted across genome, alignment, and 3D structure"
    body="Genome ↔ alignment ↔ 3D structure: the TP53 gene (RefSeq + ClinVar), the p53 ortholog alignment, and the AlphaFold structure as one connected session. It opens with the nuclear export signal motif (residues 339–350) highlighted on the genome, the alignment columns, and the folded structure."
  />

  <h2>Without the structure view</h2>
  <p>
    A session with two views instead of three is the same pattern: a gene in the
    genome view and its family in the alignment, linked by the transcript.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href={proteinLinked} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        SRC ↗
      </a>{' '}
      — the Src-family kinase alignment beside the SRC gene.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={brafV600} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        BRAF V600E ↗
      </a>{' '}
      — the RAF family on the invariant V600 codon, with a ClinVar
      pathogenic-variant track.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={tp53R248} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        TP53 R248 ↗
      </a>{' '}
      — p53 across vertebrates on the R248 hotspot, with ClinVar.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Built when the link opens</h2>
  <p>
    The sessions above carry their alignments as files. The two below carry a
    request instead, and the view builds the alignment when it opens from the
    translation of the transcript the spec names in
    <code>connectedTranscript</code>. In these and the gene sessions above, the
    plugin looks the named transcript's exon model up in the genome view's gene
    track.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href={tp53UnirefBrowser} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        TP53 UniRef cluster ↗
      </a>{' '}
      — every reference-proteome protein in UniProtKB within 50% identity of
      p53, looked up from UniProt and aligned to the transcript in the browser.
      It submits no job to any service, so it opens in seconds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={tp53PhmmerRp15} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        TP53 phmmer search ↗
      </a>{' '}
      — the same gene through a phmmer search of UniProt's representative
      proteomes at 15% similarity, the widest net EBI offers: p53's relatives
      across the tree of life. The wait is EBI's queue, from seconds to many
      minutes.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
